<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro de San Superano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1402">1402</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro de San Superano was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navarrese-company">Navarrese Company</a>, a band of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> that seized much of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> from the late 1370s. He ruled as vicar and from 1396 as prince of Achaea until his death in 1402, after which his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-ii-zaccaria">Maria II Zaccaria</a> succeeded.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pedro appears under many names in letters of his own day and in later works: he signed himself Petro de Sancto Superano, and others called him Pedro Bordo, Pierre Lebourd or the Bort de Sant Cebriá. San Superano, or Saint Superan in French, probably refers to a castle near Landiras, south-south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, and some sources say the name Bordo came from his birthplace. A work of 1907 states that he received the citizenship of Bordeaux from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the remnants of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navarrese-company">Navarrese Company</a> moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a>, probably in 1378, they were led by three captains: Mahiot de Coquerel, Berard de Varvassa and Pedro. In 1381 the Latin Emperor James of Baux made Mahiot bailiff of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, with Pedro and Berard as his captains. After James died in 1383, his successors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-naples">Charles III of Naples</a> and Ladislaus could not hold the principality, and the Company became the real power there. When Mahiot died in 1386, Pedro took his place, and on 26 July 1387 he confirmed a treaty ceding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> rights in the port of Navarino.</p><p>On 6 September 1387 Pope Urban VI claimed the principality and entrusted it to Paul Foscari, archbishop of Patras, who made Pedro vicar general. Pedro fought constantly against the Despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a>, at times with Ottoman pirates, and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nerio-i-acciaioli">Nerio I Acciaioli</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, whom he captured near Vostitsa on 10 September 1389. Late in 1394 or early in 1395 he joined the Turkish general Evrenos Beg to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akova">Akova</a>, but he was then defeated and captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> paid 50,000 hyperpers for his release.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Early in 1396 Pedro agreed to pay Ladislaus 3,000 ducats for the title of Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>; he was invested but never paid. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nicopolis">Battle of Nicopolis</a>, the threat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Sultan Bayezid I</a> drew him into alliance with the despot Theodore I Palaeologus. He defeated a Turkish army in 1399, and on 15 February 1400 Pope Boniface IX named him papal vicar and gonfalonier of Achaea. The alliance did not last, and in 1401 he raided the Venetian possessions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a>. He died the next year, leaving an infant son under the regency of his widow, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-ii-zaccaria">Maria II Zaccaria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pedro de San Superano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Absentee rule, disputed claims and the growing power of the barons and mercenary captains weakened central authority, until in 1396 the Navarrese captain <b>Pedro de San Superano</b> was recognised as prince.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44,-0.5&amp;year=1392" aria-label="Gascony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4538 605 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pedro de San Superano"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4988 875h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4988 875h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44,-0.5&amp;year=1392">What was here in 1392? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
